WEBVTT

NOTE
Landscape prototype walkthrough. These captions are also burned into the video
frame; this track exists for accessibility tooling, text extraction and reuse.

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Landscape answers one question: who else is already doing what you are planning to do?

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.500
You describe your idea in the box, in your own words. That is the only thing you have to do.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.500
Here we have asked it to compare three project-management products.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.500
It reads the companies own websites. Each page shows up as it is read, so you can watch it work.

5
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.000
One site does not allow automated reading, so it is skipped. The report will say so, rather than quietly leaving it out.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.500
Prices come first. They are taken straight from each company pricing page, so the figures are exactly what the company published.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.500
A price found somewhere else, that we could not confirm on the company own site, is kept out of the table and shown separately.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.500
Then the written summary appears, a few words at a time.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.500
The comparison table is careful about what it claims. A question mark means we looked and found nothing, which is not the same as saying they do not have it.

10
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.500
When a company does not publish its updates, the report says exactly that, and lists everywhere it looked.

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.500
Every fact links back to where it came from. Click one and you see the web page, the exact sentence, and when it was read.

12
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:52.500
Once the report is ready you can ask questions about it. Answers come back quickly, because the pages have already been read.

13
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.500
Ask something the sources do not cover, and it tells you, instead of making an answer up.

14
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.500
Without signing up you get one report a day. The report itself is complete: nothing hidden, nothing stamped across it.

15
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.500
A free account raises that to one report an hour.

16
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.500
A dollar a month gives you five an hour and as many questions as you like. The report is the same either way. Only how often you can run it changes.

17
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.500
If a competitor changes their prices, you get an email. The subject line tells you what changed.

18
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.500
And you can see the before and after side by side, both dated, so you can check it yourself.

19
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.500
There is a public help area that anyone can read. The channels for sign-up and log-in trouble accept messages from anyone, because a person who cannot log in cannot be asked to log in first.

20
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.500
Posts can be edited after you send them.

21
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.500
Behind the scenes, the owner can see how busy the service is and change any of the limits without touching code.

22
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.500
Change a number and it takes effect straight away. The right values are impossible to guess before real people are using it.

23
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:48.500
Facts you can check. Gaps it owns up to. And one thing to type to get started.
